# Djot Example

## Text Formatting

This is *bold* and this is _italic_. You can also do ~~strikethrough~~.

Superscript: E=mc^2^ and subscript: H~2~O

{=highlighted text=}

## Lists

- Unordered item
- Another item
  - Nested item

1. First
2. Second

: Term 1
  Definition of term 1

: Term 2
  Definition of term 2

## Links and Images

[Link text](https://example.com)

![Alt text](image.png)

![Image with caption](diagram.png "Caption text")

## Code

Inline `code` and blocks:

``` python
def hello():
    print("Hello, world!")
```

## Tables

| Name  | Value |
|-------|-------|
| Alpha | 1     |
| Beta  | 2     |

## Task Lists

- [x] Completed task
- [ ] Pending task

## Cross-references

See [the section on code](#code) for examples.

{#custom-anchor}
### Custom Anchor Section

Reference with [custom anchor](#custom-anchor).

## Footnotes

Here's a sentence with a footnote[^note].

[^note]: This is the footnote content.

## Blockquotes

> This is a quote.
> It can span multiple lines.

## Math

Inline: $E = mc^2$

Block:

$$
\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2} dx = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2}
$$

## Attributes

{.warning}
This paragraph has a class.

{#my-id .class1 .class2 key="value"}
This paragraph has multiple attributes.

## Djot-specific Features

### Spans and Divs

This has [some inline content]{.highlight} with attributes.

::: warning
This is a div with a class.
:::

### Raw Content

```{=html}
<div class="special">Raw HTML</div>
```

### Smart Punctuation

"Quotes" and 'apostrophes' -- dashes --- and ellipses...

### Unambiguous Syntax

Unlike Markdown, Djot has:

- Consistent emphasis rules (no edge cases)
- Clear nesting semantics
- Predictable parsing

_This is italic even_right_next to other text.

*This is bold even*right*next to other text.
